// **What a YAML parse costs against the JSON parse of the same data, and where it went.**
//
// `yaml` builds a `Json` tree, so the two parsers can be measured on one document on one machine:
// the tree they produce is the same and the difference is theirs. The roadmap measured **23x**
// through `codec` on a 1.3 MB body, which is its reason for saying that the entry which would pay
// for itself on YAML is a faster YAML parser rather than removing the tree (the tree is 8% of a
// YAML decode and a third of a JSON one).
//
// On this bench's 1.6 MB document of 20,000 orders, each parser alone in a fresh process, before
// and after the 2026-11-03 work:
//
//     yaml, a character at a time    6,535 ms     121x the json parse
//     yaml, reading runs             1,955 ms      36x
//     yaml, after 2026-11-04         1,068 ms      20x
//     json                              54 ms
//
// **The json row is the control, and it is why the 2026-11-04 row can be believed.** `json.parse`
// is native C, so interpreter work cannot touch it -- and measured again the same way on the same
// machine it is 53-54 ms, to the millisecond what it was. The yaml parse halving while its control
// does not move is the interpreter getting cheaper, not the machine getting faster.
//
// That day's work was five changes, none of them in this module: the collection guard becoming a
// root source (F267), the collection floor becoming a 4 MB setting, an enum access resolving once
// instead of per evaluation (F268), the variadic tail's type cached on its declaration, and the
// fixed compound types becoming `.rodata` constants. This file is the workload they were worth the
// most on, because it is the one that was allocation-bound to begin with -- which the paragraph
// below, written before any of them, already said.
//
// **3.3x, and four changes none of which touched how YAML is read.** In profile order:
//
//   * **`strip_comment` decoded every line into an array of one-character strings** to find a `#`
//     that almost no line has. A line with no `#` in it has no comment, whatever its quoting, so
//     one native scan answers it: 6.8% of the parse plus most of `chars`' 3.1%.
//   * **`split_key` walked every character of every line** asking `char_at` where the `:` was, and
//     recomputed `len(content)` each step. `index_of` answers in one scan and the loop now runs
//     once per `:` rather than once per character.
//   * **A quoted scalar that needs no decoding is a view onto the line.** One line, closed on it,
//     no escape in it -- which is most of them -- and the value *is* the text between the quotes,
//     where the general path built a pieces array, a `concat`, a `chars` array and a string
//     builder. A plain scalar with no continuation line skips its builder the same way.
//   * **`looks_int`/`looks_float` read `len` once** instead of per character, and the field
//     accessor `content_of` went: at twelve sites on a hot path it cost a call each to save
//     nothing.
//
// The collector went from **39.6% to 24.9%** of the parse, which is the measurement that says what
// the work was about: the parser was allocating per line and per scalar, not computing. A mild
// superlinearity went with it -- 10x the input was 13x the time and is now 10x.
//
// **Two things about measuring this, both of which produced a wrong number first.**
//
//   * **Two parsers in one loop charge the second for the first one's garbage.** The first version
//     of this file parsed the YAML and then the JSON of each size in turn, and reported the JSON
//     parse at 150 ms where it is 54 ms alone -- it was paying for the YAML tree still being live.
//     The ratio came out at 10x instead of 36x.
//   * **A warm heap hides exactly what this work removed.** The same 1.6 MB parse is ~950 ms after
//     two smaller ones have already raised the collection threshold and ~1,955 ms in a fresh
//     process. Measuring warm made the improvement look like 2.3x instead of 3.3x, because what
//     the changes stopped doing was allocating. The figures above are therefore fresh-process
//     figures, one document per run; the series below is warm after its first size, which is fine
//     for the shape it checks and wrong for an absolute number.
//
// **What is left is mostly not in this module**, which `./ziman-allocprofile` says plainly: a fresh
// parse of this document is **3.07 million allocations and 208 MB** for 1.6 MB of input, and the
// collector is 61% of it. The top sites are `type_ref` (500,014 calls, 16%: a `Type` per
// constructed value), `enum_obj_new` (400,009), `struct_obj_new` and `result_obj_new` (380,000
// each) -- which is to say **a `Result`-returning helper called six times per line costs a dozen
// heap objects per line**, and no amount of care inside `yaml.zm` changes that. `src/ast.c`'s own
// comment records that interning types was tried and abandoned for no gain.
//
// So the two directions from here are: fewer helper calls per line (the parser working over indices
// into one array rather than a struct per line, which also cuts the wrappers), or an interpreter
// that does not allocate a wrapper for every `Result` and `Option`. The second is the larger prize
// and the larger change. Inside this module the next items are `split_key`'s own 5.9%, `tokenize`'s
// 5.8% and the `tokenize`/`parse_map` pair.
//
// Run it directly: `./ziman tests/bench/yaml_scan.zm`. The series must stay linear and the ratio
// to the JSON parse must stay in the same range -- a jump in either is the regression this exists
// to catch.
use clock
use json
use math
use os
use yaml

// A block sequence of block mappings with a handful of scalars each: the shape the roadmap
// measured, and the shape a configuration file or an exported record actually has.
fn build_yaml(n: int) -> string {
    val b = mutable_string_new()
    append(b, "orders:\n")
    for i in 0..n {
        append(b, "  - id: ", str(i), "\n")
        append(b, "    sku: \"AB-", str(i), "\"\n")
        append(b, "    quantity: ", str(i % 7 + 1), "\n")
        append(b, "    price: 19.99\n")
        append(b, "    express: true\n")
    }
    return snapshot(b)
}

fn build_json(n: int) -> string {
    val b = mutable_string_new()
    append(b, "{\"orders\":[")
    for i in 0..n {
        if i > 0 {
            append(b, ",")
        }
        append(b, "{\"id\":", str(i), ",\"sku\":\"AB-", str(i), "\",\"quantity\":", str(i % 7 + 1))
        append(b, ",\"price\":19.99,\"express\":true}")
    }
    append(b, "]}")
    return snapshot(b)
}

fn yaml_ms(text: string) -> int {
    val started = clock.monotonic_millis()
    switch yaml.parse(text) {
        Ok(v) {}
        Err(e) { printf("  YAML FAILED: {}\n", e) }
    }
    return clock.monotonic_millis() - started
}

fn json_ms(text: string) -> int {
    val started = clock.monotonic_millis()
    switch json.parse(text) {
        Ok(v) {}
        Err(e) { printf("  JSON FAILED: {}\n", e) }
    }
    return clock.monotonic_millis() - started
}

fn growth_of(times: [int]) -> int {
    return math.max(times[len(times) - 1], 1) / math.max(times[0], 1)
}

// Built and measured inside one call so each document is collectable before the next is built:
// three sizes of two documents held at once is enough large-block churn for the collector to print
// a warning about it, which is noise in a bench nobody is reading for that.
fn yaml_series(counts: [int]) -> [int] {
    val times = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for n in counts {
        val text = build_yaml(n)
        val ms = yaml_ms(text)
        push(times, ms)
        printf("{} orders: yaml {} bytes in {} ms\n", str(n), str(byte_len(text)), str(ms))
    }
    return snapshot(times)
}

fn main() {
    val counts = [2000, 8000, 20000]
    val yt = yaml_series(counts)
    // **The json parse is measured once, at the largest size only, and that is deliberate.** At
    // 2,000 orders it is about 3 ms -- the clock's own resolution -- so a growth ratio taken from
    // it reads as 50x for a parser that is perfectly linear. One measurable number, for the one
    // comparison this bench makes.
    val biggest = counts[len(counts) - 1]
    val jm = json_ms(build_json(biggest))
    printf("{} orders: json in {} ms\n", str(biggest), str(jm))
    val ratio = math.max(yt[len(yt) - 1], 1) / math.max(jm, 1)
    printf(
        "10x the input cost {}x for yaml; yaml is {}x the json parse\n",
        str(growth_of(yt)),
        str(ratio)
    )
    var bad = false
    // 10x the input: linear is 10, and this was 13 before the work. 20 leaves room for a slow
    // machine without leaving room for a quadratic.
    if growth_of(yt) > 20 {
        print("FAIL: the yaml series is not linear")
        bad = true
    }
    // 70 against the measured 36, so a machine where the json parse rounds low does not fail the
    // gate; what this catches is a return to the 121x this was before 2026-11-03.
    if ratio > 70 {
        printf("FAIL: yaml is {}x the json parse, which is the shape it had before 2026-11-03\n", str(ratio))
        bad = true
    }
    if bad {
        os.exit(1)
    }
    print("ok   both parsers are linear and yaml is within a small factor of json")
}
